    "Investing in public financial markets is an intellectual paradox. The mathematical principles of successful investing are extraordinarily straightforward: diversify globally, minimize structural fees, automate contributions, reinvest dividends, and maintain a multi-decade time horizon. Yet despite this undeniable simplicity, the overwhelming majority of retail participants underperform the basic market indices they attempt to beat. This underperformance is not caused by lack of intelligence, flawed arithmetic, or inadequate software. It is caused entirely by emotional failure under psychological stress.",
