    "Automating your purchases during market crashes converts Dollar-Cost Averaging from a dry mathematical concept into a predatory wealth-building engine. When stock prices decline, your fixed weekly or monthly deposit purchases a significantly greater number of shares. You are effectively acquiring productive corporate productive capacity at a substantial clearance sale. When the broader market eventually recovers and achieves new all-time highs—as it has done following every single recession, war, and crisis in modern economic history—those discounted shares fuel explosive upward portfolio acceleration.",
    "Another crucial tactical behavior is Managing Information Consumption and App Checking Frequency. In the modern era of mobile brokerage apps, investors develop an addictive habit of checking their portfolio balances multiple times per day. Every microsecond price change becomes a source of dopamine or anxiety. Behavioral research confirms that the more frequently an investor monitors their portfolio, the more volatile their investments feel, and the higher the probability that they will make an impulsive, catastrophic reallocation error.",
